    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d759">

                <pb facs="0719" n="711" sameAs="frus:frus1940v04/pg711"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.51/7170</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The President of the Chinese Executive Yuan</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">Chiang</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                        State</hi><anchor n="34" xml:id="d759-f1"/></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Chungking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1940-12-12">December 12, 1940</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>I feel deeply grateful for the practical measures which President Roosevelt and
                    yourself have adopted in order, on the one hand to aid China, and on the other,
                    to suppress the aggressor. The loans recently announced in Washington is another
                    evidence of the readiness on the part of the United States to sustain China in
                    her struggles for complete national independence. Every further step which the
                    United States takes in regard to the Far Eastern situation tends to increase the
                    sense of gratitude of the Chinese people towards the United States, and at the
                    same time strengthens their determination to fight on till final victory is
                    won.</p>
                <p>I wish to take this opportunity to express to you my sincerest thanks.</p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d759-f1" n="34" type="source" xml:id="d759-f1-content">
                <p>Translation of telegram sent to the Secretary of State by the Chinese Ambassador
                    in a covering letter dated December 16. In this letter the Chinese Ambassador
                    expressed his desire “to join in the sentiments expressed in the General’s
                    telegram.”</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
